192 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (The cloud market combines structural barriers to entry with firm practices that may further reduce contestability. Structural features such as high fixed costs, sunk investment, and economies of scale already favour large incumbents. In addition, pricing, contractual, and technical practices including egress fees, bundling, and interoperability frictions may further raise switching costs and make rival entry or expansion more difficult.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
193 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 531 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
194 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 532 0 R
/E (Tying and Bundling: Hyperscalers discount compute services only when purchased alongside storage, identity management and networking products \(all IaaS components.This bundle extends upwards into PaaS tools like managed databases, AI services, analytics platforms, and even further into SaaS, making disaggregation economically irrational for customers. This hurts rivals such as Zoom or Heroku, who offer competitive standalone PaaS/SaaS services but cannot replicate the bundled price that combines all three products \(EC, 2025\).)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
195 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 533 0 R
/E (API & Incompatibility Barriers: AWS and Azure provide platforms with their own Application Programming Interfaces \(APIs or PaaS\) that are incompatible with rival clouds \(rival IaaS\). Migrating an application built on these services can cost millions, making switching more expensive even where a rival is cheaper \(EC, 2025\). The Commission is investigating whether this is deliberate, raising serious concerns about entrenched dominance \(EC, 2025\).)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
