/K [2]
>>
endobj
199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (The SSNIP Test and On Premise Limitations: Enterprises are unlikely to substitute away from AWS or Azure in response to a 5 to 10% price increase. While on premise IT is theoretically a substitute, it is not a credible alternative for most modern enterprises. High switching costs and technical lock in create significant demand inelasticity. The elastic scaling, integrated services, and global reach of hyperscalers cannot be replicated on premise at a comparable scale or cost.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Product Differentiation: IaaS SaaS and PaaS: Pure SaaS providers such as Salesforce or Adobe are not substitutes for IaaS providers, because they sell finished software applications rather than underlying compute, storage, and networking capacity. A customer facing a price increase in IaaS would not switch to a software platform, since these products serve entirely different functions.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 129 0 R
/T (Supply Side Substitutability)
/E (Supply Side Substitutability)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Endogenous Sunk Costs and Barriers to Entry: Unlike typical markets where high profits attract entry, entry into IaaS is blocked by massive endogenous sunk costs. Replicating the physical infrastructure of an incumbent requires years of construction and billions in capital expenditure. Consequently, market contestability is unlikely to discipline prices.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Asymmetric Entry Dynamics: The direction of entry is fundamentally asymmetric. An IaaS provider can readily move upward into PaaS and SaaS by building managed services and software applications on top of existing infrastructure. Both AWS and Azure have demonstrated this trajectory. Conversely, a PaaS or SaaS provider cannot move downward into IaaS without first crossing an enormous capital threshold.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Microsoft's transition from a PaaS focused Azure to a full stack provider illustrates that downward movement is not theoretically impossible, but it requires a balance sheet and global infrastructure base unavailable to typical competitors. Therefore, the large population of PaaS providers cannot be counted as potential entrants capable of constraining IaaS pricing. They compete only at higher layers of the stack, leaving the underlying infrastructure market without effective competitive discipline from below.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
206 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 129 0 R
/T (The Relevant Product Market)
/E (The Relevant Product Market)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
207 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Given the inelastic demand and the failure of the SSNIP test across potential substitutes, the relevant product market must be defined strictly. On premise infrastructure lacks the elastic scaling and operational expenditure model required to be a credible substitute. SaaS serves entirely distinct functional needs. Furthermore, the asymmetric barriers to entry and the technical entanglement of IaaS and PaaS necessitate grouping them together.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Therefore, the relevant market is defined exclusively as the provision of bundled public cloud infrastructure services anchored by IaaS. Providers offering solely Software as a Service or solely Platform as a Service fall)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (entirely outside this boundary. To be considered a participant in this specific market, a provider must supply foundational IaaS. Pure play PaaS, pure play SaaS, and traditional on premise IT are explicitly excluded from this definition.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (Geographic Market)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 130 0 R
/E (While cloud services are delivered globally, the relevant geographic market is the European Economic Area \(EEA\). The GDPR requires that personal data relating to EU residents be stored and processed within the EEA, meaning enterprises cannot legally substitute EEA-located cloud with infrastructure hosted elsewhere for most regulated work \(GDPR, 2026\). The GDPR therefore acts as a trade barrier creating a distinct regional market, ensuring the Commission's regulatory perimeter maps onto economic reality.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
