247 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (APIs, managed services, and internal skill sets. Under the EU Data Act, switching charges such as egress fees are being phased out, while providers are also required to facilitate switching and interoperability \(European Commission, 2026\); technical migration frictions, however, are reduced rather than eliminated outright.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (Switching-cost theory provides the demand-side foundation. Klemperer \(1995\) emphasizes that switching costs shape dynamic price competition because firms can exploit locked-in users rather than compete aggressively for marginal customers. An enterprise that has built its operations around AWS or Azure may have written applications against provider-specific APIs, embedded workloads in proprietary managed services, and trained internal teams around that environment. Migrating to another provider may therefore require code adaptation, workforce retraining, and extensive retesting of applications and security processes. Even where a rival offers a lower nominal price, the total cost of migration may render that offer commercially unattractive.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (The most policy-relevant extension is that compatibility itself may be strategic. Chen, Doraszelski, and Harrington \(2009\) model markets with network effects in which firms may choose whether to remain compatible with rivals; a firm with a large installed base may have weaker incentives to maintain compatibility where incompatibility helps preserve its advantage. Applied to cloud markets, this suggests that interoperability cannot be assumed to emerge naturally through competition alone.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 133 0 R
/T (Critical Evaluation)
/E (Critical Evaluation)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
