/P 133 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (Furthermore, the methodological choices of the investigation must be scrutinised. The CMA openly prioritised qualitative evidence because cloud procurement is complex, but that methodological choice increases the burden to triangulate stakeholder claims with measurable indicators. For the EU investigations, a strong improvement would be to exploit natural experiments: licensing-policy changes, time-limited free switching programmes, or service deprecations that create observable shocks to switching and multi-cloud behavior.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 133 0 R
/E (Lastly, regulatory timing is a major challenge. The DMA adopts a preventive, ex ante logic rather than requiring a full demonstration of harm in the ex post abuse-of-dominance sense. That may be justified in a market where installed-base advantages, ecosystem effects, and switching costs are cumulative. However, the same preventive logic also creates a risk of intervention that is too rigid for a market evolving rapidly. Multi-cloud adoption is expanding, and tools such as Kubernetes may reduce some forms of workload-level lock-in by improving portability across environments, even if they do not eliminate cloud dependence \(CNCF, 2017; CNCF, 2023\). At the same time)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 134 0 R
/E (time, AI-related demand is reshaping competitive dynamics and increasing the strategic importance of cloud infrastructure. The challenge for regulators is therefore whether intervention is warranted and if it can remain well targeted in a market whose sources of competitive advantage may shift faster than the regulatory response.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
