/T (Behavioural Remedies)
/E (Behavioural Remedies)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
266 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Behavioural remedies impose constraints on how a firm conducts itself without changing its structure. Although the commission has favoured structural remedies historically, behavioural remedies are likely to be more appropriate in this case \(Walle, 2021, p.10\). In cloud computing, the core concerns identified in this report \(tying, bundling and switching costs\) have a significant behavioural component, making this the more relevant category in our case. Typically, the behavioural remedies imposed by the DMA on digital gatekeepers include adhering to data-sharing requirements and ensuring non-discriminatory access to platforms \(Dorell and Chapman, N.A.\). These obligations are set to even the playing field and encourage competition in digital markets. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
267 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 135 0 R
/T (Market-Based Remedies)
/E (Market-Based Remedies)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
268 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 135 0 R
/E (Market-based remedies aim to improve competitive conditions by reducing barriers to entry and facilitating switching, rather than directly restructuring firms or imposing strict conduct rules. Though not always formally classified alongside structural and behavioural remedies, many DMA obligations fall within this category, including data portability requirements, transparency obligations on pricing and service terms, and interoperability mandates. In our case, these measures could be reinforced by complementary legislation such as the EU Data Act, which further reduces switching costs through interoperability standards and the phased elimination of data egress fees, which would strengthen contestability in cloud markets.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
