273 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (Evaluating remedies requires assessing whether they address the source of the problem, preserve efficiencies where possible, and can be implemented and enforced effectively in practice. In cloud computing, these criteria are complicated by the mutual reinforcement between switching costs, network effects, and bundling identified in this report. Partial remedies risk being not only incomplete but counterproductive, as firms could shift exclusionary behaviour from the targeted mechanism to an untargeted one. )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
274 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (FRAND-based remedies have been widely used across EU competition regulation to improve access to address power imbalances, although their effectiveness is context-dependent and difficult to operationalise in practice \(Mantzari, 2024\). The CMA case considered price controls on egress fees and a FRAND-based licensing package as possible remedies, while also stressing that any intervention would need flexibility, monitoring, and the ability to adapt over time \(CMA, 2025\). This is important because as Chen, Doraszelski and Harrington \(2009\) show, when firms can choose whether to make their products compatible, they can behave strategically to maintain their advantage from having more users. In a tipping equilibrium, once a firm gains an advantage, rivals have limited incentives to compete aggressively, and dominance is self-reinforced through incompatibility and network effects rather than price alone.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
275 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
276 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (Structural remedies would, in theory, address more directly the durable market power generated by network effects, but they are not the best option here. The CMA investigation concluded that structural remedies and operational separation would not be effective or proportionate in this market \(CMA, 2025, p.636\). This conclusion makes sense for our case, where, as mentioned earlier, cloud infrastructure depends on very high fixed costs and strong economies of scale, so forced separation risks eliminating efficiencies without removing the barriers to entry of the market \(Choi, 2004\).  )
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
277 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (This report therefore recommends a coordinated set of behavioural and market-based remedies, implemented simultaneously rather than sequentially. The most appropriate remedies would include:)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
