/S /P
/P 542 0 R
/E (FRAND licensing obligations for software used across rival clouds)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
281 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (While these remedies apply broadly to cloud providers, their relevance may differ across firms. In particular, FRAND-based licensing obligations are most directly targeted at Microsoft, given its control over essential software inputs such as Windows Server and SQL Server. By contrast, remedies aimed at reducing switching costs and improving interoperability are more uniformly applicable across both AWS and Azure, as both providers rely on proprietary architectures that contribute to customer lock-in. This suggests that, although a common regulatory framework can be applied, the intensity and focus of remedies may need to vary across providers.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
282 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 136 0 R
/E (Each of these remedies involves trade-offs. FRAND licensing may be difficult to monitor and enforce in practice, particularly where pricing structures are complex. Interoperability obligations can reduce switching costs but may limit incentives for product differentiation. Similarly, banning egress fees may lead providers to adjust pricing XXXX)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (elsewhere, reducing the overall effectiveness of the intervention. These challenges reinforce the need for coordinated and adaptable remedies.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (Combined, these remedies target the three mechanisms identified in our analysis \(network effects, switching costs, and bundling\) while preserving the efficiencies associated with the scale. Since AWS and Azure currently fall outside of the quantitative thresholds of article 3 due to the B2B nature of cloud services, the EC should prioritise the gatekeeper designation via the qualitative route under Article 3\(9\) as a necessary first step before the full Articles 5 and 6 toolkit can be applied.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
