286 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (This report has assessed whether AWS and Azure qualify as gatekeepers of cloud computing services under the Digital Markets Act, and whether the competitive concerns identified in the European cloud market warrant regulatory intervention.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
287 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (The European cloud infrastructure market is characterised by a highly concentrated oligopoly in which three US hyperscalers collectively account for approximately 70% of regional revenues. This concentration is structural rather than temporary. European providers' share collapsed from 29% to 15% between 2017 and 2024, even as the overall market grew sixfold, consistent with Motta's \(2004\) thesis that persistent market share amid growth is the definitive indicator of entrenched dominance rather than competitive equilibrium.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
288 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
290 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 137 0 R
/E (This asymmetry has direct consequences for remedies. For Azure, qualitative designation unlocks the full Articles 5 and 6 toolkit. For AWS, targeted behavioural intervention under competition law is the more proportionate instrument. Across both providers, as Chen, Doraszelski and Harrington \(2009\) demonstrate, financial and technical remedies must be implemented simultaneously, reducing egress fees without mandating interoperability risks leaving the tipping dynamic intact. As cloud infrastructure becomes increasingly critical to AI development and Europe's strategic autonomy, the Commission's investigations represent a necessary intervention. However, their effectiveness depends on recognising that AWS and Azure present distinct regulatory challenges that demand differentiated legal treatment.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
